<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">11 September 1893 · Chicago</p><h1 class="sp-title">Vivekananda at the Parliament of Religions</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swami-vivekananda">Swami Vivekananda</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>The World's Parliament of Religions, held in Chicago alongside the World's Columbian Exposition, brought together representatives of many of the world's faiths in a gathering without precedent. On its opening day Swami Vivekananda, a thirty-year-old Hindu monk from Bengal and a disciple of the mystic Ramakrishna, rose to give a short address of welcome.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>Sisters and Brothers of America, it fills my heart with joy unspeakable to rise in response to the warm and cordial welcome which you have given us.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Response to Welcome, World's Parliament of Religions, Chicago, 11 September 1893.</p><div class="sp-full"><a class="sp-full-link" href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Complete_Works_of_Swami_Vivekananda/Volume_1/Addresses_at_The_Parliament_of_Religions/Response_to_Welcome" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the complete text<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a><span class="sp-full-src">Wikisource, Complete Works of Swami Vivekananda</span></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>His opening words were met with a long ovation, and he became the best-known speaker of the parliament. He lectured across the United States and Britain, founded the Ramakrishna Mission after his return to India in 1897, and became a major influence on Hindu reform, on Indian nationalism and on Western interest in Vedanta and yoga. His birthday is celebrated in India as National Youth Day.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramakrishna-mission">Ramakrishna Mission</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a></div></div></section>
